---
import Clip from '../../../components/Clip.astro'
import Explainer from '../../../components/Explainer.astro'
import { diagrams } from '../../../data/diagrams.js'
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { streamExample } from '../../../lib/api-reference.js'
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths.js'

const otherDevice = `import { createBrowserViewer, launchBrowser } from '@suitecut/browser-control'

const browser = await launchBrowser({ executablePath, headless: true, pixelFormat: 'bgra' })
const page = await browser.newPage()
const viewer = await createBrowserViewer({ browser, page, network: true, resolutionMode: 'auto' })
console.log('Open on any device on this network:', viewer.url)

// Agent steps wait while a person has control.
await viewer.runAgent(() => page.goto('https://www.ebay.com/'))`
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Live streaming"
  description="Stream a headless browser to your phone or another computer, or publish it to RTMP or RTMPS."
  section="streaming"
>
  <h1>Native live streaming</h1>
  <p>
    A suitecut browser streams itself. Both ways below are part of the engine and its SDK, with no
    extension, relay or cloud service: watch and take over from another device you hold, or publish
    the page, with its sound, to an audience.
  </p>
  <h2 id="other-devices">To your phone or another computer</h2>
  <p>
    <code>createBrowserViewer</code> serves a live view of the engine's own frames. With
    <code>network: true</code> the printed link opens on any phone, tablet or computer on the same network;
    it fits small screens and takes touch. <b>Take control</b> pauses the agent,
    <b>Resume AI</b> hands the page back, and any number of screens can watch at once.
  </p>
  <Clip
    name="phone-viewer"
    title="A phone takes over an agent's eBay search, then hands it back."
    portrait
  >
    {' '}The page resizes to the screen in control, so eBay shows its phone layout.
  </Clip>
  <CodeBlock code={otherDevice} />
  <p>
    The link carries its own key, and requests without it are refused. It is plain HTTP, so share it
    on networks you trust; without <code>network</code> the viewer stays on this machine. From the command
    line, <code>browser-control view https://www.ebay.com/ --network</code> does the same. The <a
      href={sitePath('/examples#phone')}>full example</a
    > runs an agent alongside it.
  </p>
  <h2 id="publish-a-page">To an audience, over RTMP</h2>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.streaming} />
  <CodeBlock code={streamExample} />
  <h2 id="stream-options">Stream options</h2>
  <p>
    Set an RTMP or RTMPS URL, output size, output frame rate, boolean audio, a fixed
    <code>bitrateKbps</code>, <code>captureLatencyMs</code> and an optional <code>reconnect</code>
    policy. Output rates can be fractional, such as 29.97.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>selectSource()</code> switches to another page in the same native browser helper, for example
    one from <code>context.newPage()</code>. It needs matching dimensions and pixel format; capture
    rates may differ. Switching to a page in a different helper isn't supported. Broadcasts also
    support <code>pause()</code>, <code>resume()</code> and <code>cancel()</code>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="page-audio">Page audio</h2>
  <p>
    Audio comes from CEF callbacks as 48 kHz stereo PCM. It is disabled by default. Timestamped
    buffering aligns it with the video timeline; missing audio contributes silence.
  </p>
  <h2 id="bitrate-and-health">Bitrate and health</h2>
  <p>
    Bitrate is fixed: <code>bitrateKbps</code> accepts 100 to 50,000 and defaults to 4500.
    <code>adaptiveBitrate: true</code> and <code>onDiagnostic</code> are rejected.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>await broadcast.health()</code> and <code>status()</code> return native counters for submitted,
    held and encoded frames, muxed bytes, reconnect attempts and
    <code>maxCaptureToPipeMs</code>. Muxed bytes don't prove the remote server received them.
  </p>
  <h2 id="reconnect-and-stop">Reconnect and stop</h2>
  <p>
    Reconnect is off unless you pass
    <code>reconnect: &#123; maxAttempts, initialDelayMs, maxDelayMs &#125;</code>. It allows one or
    more attempts per outage, with defaults of 3 attempts, 250 ms and 5000 ms. Without a policy, the
    publisher makes one attempt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Closing the page, context or browser gracefully finalizes the publisher. Crashes and forced
    termination surface on <code>failure</code>. Your app owns its source and should watch both the
    source and the broadcast.
  </p>
  <h2 id="live-effects">Live effects</h2>
  <p>
    Browser-rendered highlights and captions reach captured frames. Offline zoom and the renderer's
    narration mix do not transform a concurrent broadcast.
  </p>
  <h2 id="verify-locally">Verify locally</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    label="Terminal"
    code={'pnpm test:native:cadence\npnpm test:native:stream\npnpm test:native:reconnect'}
  />
  <p>
    The stream verifier needs the built native browser and checks decoded received media and source
    replacement. <code>test:native:reconnect</code> covers refused connections, listener replacement and
    closure during retries. Publishing has known audio dropouts and low-rate Opus startup failures. Streaming
    throughput at 120 FPS remains unverified; measured recording throughput is on the <a
      href={sitePath('/docs/performance')}>performance page</a
    >.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
